---
description: >
  When source code is available as a local file, always attempt to link
  runtime issues (slow stages, errors, skew) back to specific code locations.
  Applies to all Spark job analysis when the user provides a source file path.
alwaysApply: true
---

When source code is available (the user provides a local file path), always
connect runtime observations to code:

1. **Map stages to code**: Use SQL plan node names and stage descriptions to
   identify which transformation in the code produced each stage. Include
   file name and line number.

2. **Map errors to code**: When logs contain stack traces referencing user code
   (not Spark internals), identify the exact file and line. If the error is in
   a UDF, identify the UDF definition.

3. **Map regressions to diffs**: When comparing runs, if a regression is
   detected and the user provides a previous version of the file, diff the
   two versions and check if any code change aligns with the performance
   difference. Specifically check:
   - Changed join conditions or predicates
   - Added/removed caching
   - Changed repartition/coalesce
   - New or modified UDFs
   - Changed data sources or file formats
   - Modified Spark configuration in code

4. **Format**: Always present code links as:
   `<file_path>:<line_number>` — `<brief description of the relevant code>`

If source code is not provided, explicitly state: "Source code was not
provided for this analysis. Recommendations are based on runtime data only.
Provide the job file path to enable code-level correlation."
